things up, when we downsize or declutter or lose a treasured possession? And how has the rise of the digital age and the sharing economy change the way people think about the importance of possessions? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. My guest today is Dr. Russell Belk, a distinguished research professor and Craft Foods Canada chair in marketing at the Shulik School of Business at York University in Ontario. Dr. Belk studies the meaning of possessions collecting gift-giving, sharing, and materialism. His research interests include understanding what our possessions mean to us, how different cultures
当我们精简生活、清理杂物或失去一件珍贵的物品时，会发生什么？数字时代的兴起和共享经济如何改变了人们对拥有物重要性的看法？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》，这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯。我今天的嘉宾是罗素·贝尔克博士，他是约克大学舒立克商学院的市场营销学杰出研究教授和加拿大克拉夫特食品公司主席。贝尔克博士研究拥有物、收藏、赠礼、分享和物质主义的意义。他的研究兴趣包括理解我们的拥有物对我们意味着什么，不同文化如何

4
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:39.000
regard consumption, and how we relate to each other through possessions. Dr. Belk is the author of more than 650 journal articles and books. He is a past president of the Association for Consumer Research and has received numerous awards for his work, including being elected a fellow of the Royal Society of Canada. Dr. Belk, thank you for joining us today. Thank you for having me. It's pleasure. One of your most influential research papers is called Possessions and the Extended Self. So let's start with that. What does it mean to think of our possessions as extensions of our self? Well, if you look at something like a musical instrument or a mechanical aid like a hammer or a screwdriver, these literally extend the body.
看待消费，以及我们如何通过拥有物与他人建立联系。贝尔克博士撰写了650多篇期刊文章和书籍。他曾任消费者研究协会主席，并因其工作获得了无数奖项，包括当选为加拿大皇家学会院士。贝尔克博士，感谢您今天加入我们的节目。谢谢邀请，这是我的荣幸。您最具影响力的研究论文之一是《拥有物与扩展的自我》。那么，让我们从这里开始。将我们的拥有物视为自我的延伸意味着什么？嗯，如果你看看乐器或像锤子、螺丝刀这样的机械辅助工具，它们实际上扩展了身体。

5
00:02:39.000 --> 00:03:14.000
They allow us to do things that we can't do with just our voice or with just our hands. But when we get into symbolic possessions, these are things that represent us. I'm in a household where we just got a new puppy. And the puppy is being trained. and sometimes it does well and sometimes it doesn't do well. And rather like our children, if they do something wonderful, we're related. If they do something terrible, we are sort of shame that it's our fault in a sense because these children, these companion animals are extensions of ourselves.
它们让我们能够做一些仅靠声音或双手无法完成的事情。但当我们谈到象征性的拥有物时，这些东西代表了我们。我住的家里刚养了一只小狗。小狗正在接受训练，有时表现得很好，有时则不然。就像我们的孩子一样，如果它们做了很棒的事情，我们会感到欣慰；如果它们做了糟糕的事情，我们会在某种意义上感到羞愧，觉得这是我们的错，因为这些孩子、这些伴侣动物是我们自我的延伸。

6
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:59.000
And so in that sense, we wax and we wane depending upon how our possessions prosper. Imagine, for example, that you just got a new piece of clothing and you spill tomato soup all over it. Or you just get a new automobile and you have a little fender scraper. It's almost like your body is being scraped or damaged or soiled. And so in that sense, in a nutshell or in a capsule, these are possessions that not only extend what we can do, but they represent us. they have symbolic meaning. Many people think of possessions as things that we own in a physical or a legal sense, but your definition is broader than that.
因此，从这个意义上说，我们的情绪会随着拥有物的状况而起伏。例如，想象一下你刚买了一件新衣服，却不小心把番茄汤洒在了上面。或者你刚买了一辆新车，却发生了一点刮擦。这感觉就像你的身体被刮伤、损坏或弄脏了一样。所以，简而言之，这些拥有物不仅扩展了我们的能力，还代表了我们，它们具有象征意义。许多人认为拥有物是我们从物理或法律意义上拥有的东西，但您的定义比这更广泛。

7
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:43.000
How far can the definition of possession stretch? Well, that's a good question. And it's one that has more salience, as you pointed out in your introduction, because of the sharing economy that we have gotten into and because of the digital economy that we're all a part of. And so we can own things that we do not possess and we can feel that we own something that is intangible. So students in a classroom, for example, that come and sit in the same non-assigned seat every day or every week, come to think about it as their seat. And if someone else is in it, they may be perturbed enough to actually ask the person to move.
拥有物的定义可以延伸到多远？嗯，这是一个好问题。正如你在介绍中所指出的，由于我们进入了共享经济，并且我们都是数字经济的一部分，这个问题变得更加突出。因此，我们可以拥有我们并不占有的东西，也可以感觉到我们拥有某种无形的东西。例如，教室里的学生每天或每周都坐在同一个非指定的座位上，他们会认为那是“他们的”座位。如果别人坐在那里，他们可能会感到不安，甚至会要求那个人离开。

8
00:04:43.000 --> 00:05:29.000
or they may slink off and sit in another seat. So they're assuming that they own that possession, even though they don't own it. Or to take another example, children in the home, unless it's a very unusual home, don't have to knock on the door and ask if they can come in or whether they can sit down on the sofa. Even though these are possessions that are legally not theirs, they are de facto shared possessions. The family shares them. And unless it was a very unusual family, when you left home, you did not receive an itemized bill of everything your parents had spent on you, which they expected you to repay. And so this is sharing within the family, which is where the greatest amount of sharing takes place.
或者他们可能会默默走开，坐在另一个座位上。所以他们假设自己拥有那个拥有物，尽管他们并没有法律上的所有权。或者再举个例子，家里的孩子，除非是一个非常特殊的家庭，否则不需要敲门询问是否可以进来，或者是否可以坐在沙发上。尽管这些在法律上不属于他们，但它们是事实上的共享拥有物。家庭共享这些东西。而且，除非是一个非常特殊的家庭，否则当你离家时，你并没有收到一份父母为你花费的所有物品的明细账单，并被要求偿还。所以这是家庭内部的分享，这是分享发生最频繁的地方。

9
00:05:29.000 --> 00:06:08.000
Now, when we go to the so-called sharing economy with Airbnb and Uber and other forms of sharing, we're not really sharing. We're doing something closer to short-term rental. And so it's not at all the feeling of ours rather than mine in yours. There's still a mine in yours. And people don't take care of possessions that they're renting on a short-term basis the same way they would the possession that they actually owned. So they may drive the car faster or they may leave the house or the home in less of a pristine condition than they might their own if they owned it.
现在，当我们进入所谓的共享经济，比如Airbnb、Uber和其他形式的分享时，我们并不是真的在分享。我们所做的事情更接近于短期租赁。所以，这根本不是“我们的”感觉，而不是“我的”和“你的”。这里仍然存在“我的”和“你的”之分。人们不会像对待自己真正拥有的物品那样去爱护短期租赁的物品。所以他们可能会开得更快，或者把房子或家留下的状况不如他们自己拥有时那样整洁。

10
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:55.000
And so we feel differently depending upon whether something is truly ours or whether it's shared. Now, when we get to the digital economy, it's also tricky. We used to, if you can see behind me, this I know it was going out just with audio, but if you can see behind me, there's a bookcase full of books. And as an academic, I have for many years now hung on to tangible physical books. but now part of my library is digital, and so I can just call it up on my computer. But there's a difference. When I shut my computer, I no longer have access to that book unless I can call it up on my smartphone, perhaps.
因此，根据某样东西是真正属于我们的还是共享的，我们的感受是不同的。现在，当我们谈到数字经济时，情况也很棘手。过去，如果你能看到我身后——我知道这只是音频节目，但如果你能看到我身后——有一个装满书的书架。作为一名学者，多年来我一直坚持保留实体的纸质书。但现在我的图书馆有一部分是数字化的，所以我可以在电脑上随时调取。但两者是有区别的。当我关掉电脑时，我就无法再访问那本书了，除非我能在智能手机上调取它。

11
