00:11:37.000 --> 00:12:08.000
And we tell our parents or show a friend, here's my collection of stuff. And they say, well, what kind of stuff? And that's not a collection. This is just some pebbles and sticks and other objects that you found on the ground. And it's not a collection because a collection has to obey some rules. And it should be in a singular category. And you should have one of a kind. You shouldn't hoard things by having lots and lots of the same thing.
我们告诉父母或向朋友展示：“这是我的收藏品。”他们会说：“什么种类的东西？这不算收藏。这只是一些你在地上捡到的鹅卵石、树枝和其他物体。”这不算收藏，因为收藏必须遵循一些规则。它应该属于一个单一的类别。你应该拥有独一无二的东西。你不应该通过囤积大量相同的东西来收藏。

19
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:49.000
And you should have some sort of sense of order and some sort of boundaries to this collection. And anyway, we learned to make good collections. good collections follow some sort of rules so we can describe what it is that we collect. Now, interestingly, collections often don't start out in a purposeful way that I'm going to collect X or Y or Z, but rather someone gives us a duck, and we know, not a real duck, a duck that's ceramic perhaps, and we have some other ducks made of wood or plaster in our home, and we look around and I say, I must be a duck collector. There's something about ducks that have adhered to me.
而且你应该对这个收藏有一种秩序感和某种界限。总之，我们学会了如何进行好的收藏。好的收藏遵循某种规则，这样我们就能描述我们收藏的是什么。有趣的是，收藏往往不是以一种有目的的方式开始的，比如“我要收藏X、Y或Z”，而是有人送给我们一只鸭子，我们家里可能还有一些木头或石膏做的鸭子，我们环顾四周，然后说：“我一定是个鸭子收藏家。”有些关于鸭子的东西已经吸引了我。

20
00:12:49.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Nevertheless, when we begin to collect them, we find some joy in that, joy in bringing order, joy in creating a collection that it adheres somehow. It's greater than the sum of its parts because it is a part of a collection. And so one of the joys is that we have sort of a collection of a little world that we can control. We may not have control of our careers or our behavior on our jobs, and we don't have control of much of the exterior world, but we do have control of this collection. And it brings us joy when we're able to expand it and we're able to improve upon it. And for that matter, other collectors may admire us. Now, I should point out that the type of collection I've been talking about is one that does have some sense of coherence. And we could think of an old stamp collection or coin collection.
尽管如此，当我们开始收藏它们时，我们从中找到了一些乐趣，带来秩序的乐趣，创造一个以某种方式粘合在一起的收藏的乐趣。它大于其各部分的总和，因为它是一个收藏的一部分。所以乐趣之一是，我们拥有一个我们可以控制的小世界。我们可能无法控制我们的职业或我们在工作中的表现，我们也无法控制外部世界的大部分，但我们确实可以控制这个收藏。当我们能够扩展它并改进它时，它会给我们带来快乐。而且，其他收藏家可能会钦佩我们。现在，我应该指出，我一直在谈论的这种收藏是具有某种连贯性的。我们可以想到旧邮票收藏或硬币收藏。

21
00:13:44.000 --> 00:14:38.000
And those were things where you could sort of fill in the boxes and you would know when it was complete. You had the entire series of coins of country A, B, or C, or you had filled up your stamp book with the stamps that were listed. But another type of, and we still have that sort of collection, but another sort of collection is one where we have a category, like we're going to be an art collector. But we're not confining ourselves to a particular genre, a particular period, a particular artist. We're just giving things that strike our eye. And, you know, someone could have, you know, a passion for yellow things, and they collect yellow things. Or they may have a passion for a particular style of painting or a particular medium in which the artist is working. And so they collect within a cat.
那些是可以填满方格的东西，你会知道什么时候它完成了。你拥有了A、B或C国的所有硬币系列，或者你已经用列出的邮票填满了你的邮票册。但另一种类型——我们仍然有那种收藏，但另一种收藏是，我们有一个类别，比如“我要成为一名艺术收藏家”。但我们并不局限于特定的流派、特定的时期、特定的艺术家。我们只是收集那些吸引我们眼球的东西。你知道，有人可能对黄色的东西有热情，他们就收集黄色的东西。或者他们可能对某种绘画风格或艺术家正在使用的某种媒介有热情。所以他们在类别内进行收藏。

22
00:14:38.000 --> 00:15:12.000
But within that category, anything goes. And so they're not filling in the narrow boxes that a type A collector would be. A type A collector is a little bit like we refer to someone with a type A personality. Perhaps a bit ain't already to hit those. I might say they're collecting, but they're constrained by rules rather than letting their imagination and desires rule. Well, I was just going to ask a question here. I'm sorry to interrupt you, but it got me thinking because I know you've defined two types of collecting, aesthetic and taxonomic. It seems that you're talking about the two right now. Could you
但在该类别内，任何东西都可以。所以他们并没有填满A型收藏家会填满的狭窄方格。A型收藏家有点像我们所说的A型人格。也许有点急于求成。我可能会说他们在收藏，但他们受到规则的约束，而不是让他们的想象力和欲望来主导。嗯，我正要在这里问一个问题。抱歉打断你，但这让我开始思考，因为我知道你定义了两种收藏类型：审美型和分类型。你现在似乎正在谈论这两种。你能

23
00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:50.000
explain how those are different? Yeah, taxonomic collection has, think of it as a two-by-two matrix, and we have some dimensions that we're trying to fulfill, and we're trying to collect one thing in each of those four boxes. And when we have it complete, we've completed the collection. We don't want to put more things in one of those boxes because we'd be duplicating in that case. We'd be hoarding rather than collecting. So that's a type A taxonomic collector. The type B aesthetic collector is looking for things that please the eye
解释一下它们有什么不同吗？是的，分类收藏，把它想象成一个二乘二的矩阵，我们有一些想要满足的维度，我们试图在每一个方格里收集一样东西。当我们完成它时，我们就完成了收藏。我们不想在那些方格里放更多的东西，因为那样我们就是在重复。那样就是囤积而不是收藏。所以那是A型分类收藏家。B型审美收藏家正在寻找那些取悦眼睛

24
00:15:50.000 --> 00:16:28.000
or the ear or whatever it is that we're collecting within that sensory mode. Smells could be another thing that we might collect, for example, or places that we've traveled. But once we have that category, we're not saying, well, I'm going to collect all of the European countries by visiting all of the European countries. In that case, it would be more taxonomic. We're just going to say, I'm going to go to the places that interest me in the world. And I've never been to Peru, and so I think I'll go to Peru, and I think I'll go to Machu Picchu while I'm there. And so those are some of the things that might interest us in that case.
或耳朵，或者我们在该感官模式内收集的任何东西。例如，气味可能是我们可以收集的另一件事，或者我们去过的地方。但一旦我们有了那个类别，我们并不是说：“好吧，我要通过访问所有欧洲国家来收集所有欧洲国家。”在这种情况下，它会更偏向分类。我们只是说：“我要去世界上让我感兴趣的地方。”我从来没去过秘鲁，所以我想我会去秘鲁，我想在去那里的时候去马丘比丘。所以这些是在那种情况下可能让我们感兴趣的一些事情。

25
00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:56.000
but I also want to go to Australia. I also want to see the kangaroos. So this is just following our curiosity or our aesthetic sense rather than trying to complete and take off the boxes. Now, you've said that collecting can be beneficial for some people. How so? Well, it can be therapeutic. It can be a way of doing something that we feel is meaningful.
但我还想去澳大利亚。我还想看袋鼠。所以这只是跟随我们的好奇心或审美感，而不是试图完成和勾选方格。现在，你说过收藏对某些人来说是有益的。怎么说呢？嗯，它可以是治疗性的。它可以是一种做我们觉得有意义的事情的方式。

26
00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:36.000
Even though to the outside world, it may not. appear meaningful. We once did a visit to, and this was on a project where I was with a group of others, to Mr. Ed's Elephant Museum and Roadside Attraction near Gettysburg, Pennsylvania. And we stopped because we saw this huge bag of peanuts out in front. And it turned out that Mr. Ed, like some others in the Elephant Collector Society or the collectors of Alphantiana, shared. and that was a joy in collecting anything related to elephants, and it could be a poster, it could be a piece of music, it could be an oil can that was shaped like an elephant.
尽管对外界来说，它可能看起来并不有意义。我们曾经参观过——这是一个我和其他人一起参与的项目——位于宾夕法尼亚州葛底斯堡附近的埃德先生大象博物馆和路边景点。我们停下来是因为我们看到了前面那一大袋花生。结果发现，埃德先生像“大象收藏家协会”或其他“大象纪念品收藏家”中的其他人一样，分享着这种快乐。收藏任何与大象有关的东西都是一种乐趣，它可以是一张海报、一段音乐、一个形状像大象的油罐。

27
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:58.000
And he presented this to the public, and he had a shop where he also sold things, but that was entirely separate from the collections, and things would never go back and forth. But he said, in all honesty, standing in front of his roadside attraction and museum, history will someday stand in awe, of me and what I've accomplished here.
他把这些展示给公众，他有一个商店，他也卖东西，但这与收藏完全分开，东西永远不会来回流动。但他诚实地说，站在他的路边景点和博物馆前：“历史终有一天会敬畏我，以及我在这里所取得的成就。”

28
00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:38.000
And so he felt in a vague way that he was contributing to art and or science by this objective that he had to collect all sorts of elephants. And because there were other elephant collectors that reinforced him in that belief, because other people stopped by the museum, he got some reinforcement for that idea. And who's to say that he's wrong? It may be unlikely that he's going to go down in the books of science or art as a, famous collector because this is a collection that would be regarded as trivial by many collectors who are really into their collection at the high end and hoping to profit from it.
所以他模糊地感觉到，通过他收集各种大象的目标，他正在为艺术和/或科学做出贡献。因为有其他大象收藏家在那种信念上支持他，因为其他人停下来参观博物馆，他从那个想法中得到了一些强化。谁能说他是错的呢？他不太可能作为一位著名的收藏家载入科学或艺术史册，因为这是一个会被许多真正投入高端收藏并希望从中获利的收藏家视为琐碎的收藏。

29
00:18:38.000 --> 00:19:15.000
It used to be that most collectors might say something, well, like, this is for my retirement. I'm saving up and I will cash this out once I'm retired. But that was a pipe dream because a few other people wanted to buy these collections, at least few other people that were encountered. Now that we're able to go online to eBay and other similar equivalents, it's easier to sell a collection. So there's greater truth to that promise if we're collecting especially at the high end. But collecting brings different joys to different people. And part of it is, I think, just this ability to control a small world.
过去，大多数收藏家可能会说：“这是为了我的退休。我正在存钱，退休后我会把这些变现。”但这只是白日梦，因为很少有其他人想买这些收藏品，至少很少遇到其他人。现在我们能够上网到eBay和其他类似的平台，出售收藏品变得更容易了。所以，如果我们进行高端收藏，那个承诺的真实性就更高了。但收藏给不同的人带来不同的快乐。我认为，其中一部分只是这种控制一个小世界的能力。

30
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:56.000
So you mentioned the Internet in places like eBay where you can sell collections. So the Internet has clearly changed collecting. Has it made it more or less challenging for people, do you think? I think it's made it less challenging, in a sense, although you can refine your collection so that becomes more esoteric and equally difficult. But there are people like William Gibson who gave us the book that became the Matrix series of movies. And he said he loved watches, but he didn't want to collect because it was too much work. and would take him all over the world until eBay came along.
所以你提到了互联网和像eBay这样可以出售收藏品的地方。所以互联网显然改变了收藏。你认为它让人们变得更具挑战性还是不那么具挑战性了？我认为在某种意义上它变得不那么具挑战性了，尽管你可以细化你的收藏，使其变得更加深奥，同样困难。但像威廉·吉布森（William Gibson）这样的人，他给了我们那本后来成为《黑客帝国》系列电影的书。他说他喜欢手表，但他不想收藏，因为那太费力了，而且会让他跑遍世界各地，直到eBay出现。

31
00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:33.000
And that made it easy enough to collect and actually find the watches that he wanted to enter into this collection. And so, as I say, it makes it easier. But if we develop even narrower rules of what we're trying to collect, we can still present ourselves with perhaps an equal challenge to what we might have had previously. So there's a saying that he who has the most toys wins. And that got me thinking, are men more inclined to collect than women? Have you found any differences between the sexes when it comes to collecting stuff? Historically, yes.
这使得收藏变得足够容易，并且实际上找到了他想要进入这个收藏的手表。所以，正如我所说，它让事情变得更容易了。但如果我们制定更狭窄的规则来决定我们要收藏什么，我们仍然可以给自己带来与以前可能遇到的同等挑战。所以有一句谚语说：“拥有最多玩具的人赢了。”这让我开始思考，男性是否比女性更倾向于收藏？在收藏东西方面，你有没有发现性别之间的任何差异？历史上，是的。

32
00:20:33.000 --> 00:21:09.000
Men have been more inclined to or more able to collect. The men historically have had more control of the wealth than women have. Also, when men collect, it's regarded as it must be a purposeful and meaningful collection. When women collect, it's just you have all of this silly stuff. Why do you have these things? It's regarded more frivolously. And that's obviously a sexist bias, but I think it still persists in society. It may be that in patriarchal societies, men have more control of the pocketbook.
男性一直更倾向于或更有能力收藏。历史上，男性比女性拥有更多的财富控制权。此外，当男性收藏时，它被认为一定是一个有目的和有意义的收藏。当女性收藏时，它只是“你拥有所有这些愚蠢的东西。你为什么有这些东西？”它被认为更轻浮。这显然是一种性别偏见，但我认为它在社会中仍然存在。可能在父权制社会中，男性对钱包有更多的控制权。

33
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:56.000
They have more control of the spaces in which to exhibit their wealth. We once encountered a man and woman who both had collections. And he had a collection of fire brigade equipment from historically different eras in society. She had a collection of mice replicas. And sadly, that's sort of telling that she was collecting the miniature, delicate thing. He was collecting the rugged, masculine. They were clicking to the stereotypes of their genders, if you will. And so there is some gender bias in the sense of not only whether we collect, but what we collect as well.
他们对展示财富的空间有更多的控制权。我们曾经遇到过一对男女，他们都有收藏。他收藏了历史上不同时代的消防队设备。她收藏了老鼠复制品。可悲的是，这说明了她正在收集微小的、精致的东西。他正在收集粗犷的、阳刚的东西。他们符合了他们性别的刻板印象。所以，不仅在是否收藏方面，而且在收藏什么方面，确实存在一些性别偏见。

