Well, it sounds as if you have encountered many, many different types of collections. I'm wondering if there are any that stick out in your mind as being among the most unusual or surprising. Well, there was a woman who collected dirt, which struck us as a little bit unusual. But she said, don't you see, this was dirt from Jerusalem. And this is dirt from Peru. And she had a nomenclature which would defy the eye. We've also had encountered people that collect ineffable sorts of objects like the types of planes that they've seen or the types of trains that they have seen are ridden on. And so these are things that like bird, what do you call it, bird watchers, are collecting birds in a sense.
嗯，听起来你遇到过许多许多不同类型的收藏。我想知道是否有任何收藏让你印象深刻，觉得是最不寻常或最令人惊讶的。嗯，有一位女士收集泥土，这让我们觉得有点不寻常。但她说：“你没看出来吗？这是来自耶路撒冷的泥土。这是来自秘鲁的泥土。”她有一套令人眼花缭乱的命名法。我们也遇到过人们收集一些难以言喻的物体，比如他们见过的飞机类型，或者他们见过或乘坐过的火车类型。所以这些东西就像观鸟者一样，在某种意义上是在收集鸟类。

35
00:22:46.000 --> 00:23:23.000
but they're not collecting the physical birds. They're just collecting notebooks, and perhaps in some cases, photographs of the birds. And so we can collect experiences as well as tangible sorts of things. I have to out myself as a birder and admit that I do have one of those lists. Let's go back to more on what's happening in the world around possessions and the changing ways that we're viewing them. I've heard you say in another interview that you prefer the term collaborative consumption to sharing economy.
但他们并不是在收集实体的鸟。他们只是在收集笔记本，在某些情况下，可能是鸟类的照片。所以我们不仅可以收集实体的东西，还可以收集体验。我必须承认自己是一名观鸟者，并承认我确实有那样的一份清单。让我们回到关于拥有物周围正在发生的事情以及我们看待它们的方式的变化上。我在另一次采访中听到你说，你更喜欢“协作消费”这个词，而不是“共享经济”。

36
00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:57.000
Why is that? Okay, collaborative consumption is the term that I'm using for the so-called sharing economy. And when we engage in the sharing economy, we're not really owning things. We are collaborating and using them. And so, for example, you might subscribe to rent the runway. and you pay a monthly fee, in an exchange for that, you can get as many handbags or designer outfits as you like. It's just that you have to return the former one before you get the next one sent to you.
为什么呢？好的，“协作消费”是我用来指代所谓“共享经济”的术语。当我们参与共享经济时，我们并不是真的在拥有东西。我们是在协作并使用它们。例如，你可能会订阅“Rent the Runway”。你支付月费，作为交换，你可以得到尽可能多的手提包或设计师服装。只是你必须在收到下一件之前归还前一件。

37
00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:40.000
And so in this sense, you look like you have a fabulous wardrobe, but actually you just have access to rent the runway as long as you pay your subscription fees. That's not like a collection of actual handbags that you're. you can keep on a shelf and they will remain there. And it gives you a sense of having the entire collection rather than having access to it. Now, again, there may be a generational thing here. It may be that those of us who were not born digital think of these tangible things as somehow more real and more rewarding and more substantive and more important than the things we can access.
所以从这个意义上说，你看起来拥有一个很棒的衣橱，但实际上你只是拥有了“Rent the Runway”的访问权限，只要你支付订阅费。这不像你真正拥有的手提包收藏，你可以把它们放在架子上，它们会一直留在那里。它让你有一种拥有整个收藏的感觉，而不是拥有访问权限。现在，这里可能存在代际差异。可能我们这些非数字原住民认为这些实物在某种程度上比我们可以访问的东西更真实、更有回报、更充实、更重要。

38
00:24:40.000 --> 00:25:38.000
But if you think about it, if you are what you access rather than you are, are what you have, you have a much broader sense of the world. If you don't have to own a vacation home, but you have access to vacation homes around the world, you're in a sense a broader sort of person that you can take advantage of more cultures and more places and so forth. Now, people get locked into sharing resources like rental unit, not rental units, but not share. A time share. time share apartments. And eventually they get tired of that and want to get out of it and all of the travel that would be involved in the paperwork and so forth is something that they don't enjoy. But nevertheless, in principle, you have access to a much greater array of things via the sharing economy than you would if you had to buy those things all on your own.
但如果你仔细想想，如果你是“你所访问的东西”而不是“你所拥有的东西”，你对世界的看法会更广阔。如果你不需要拥有一处度假屋，但你可以访问世界各地的度假屋，从某种意义上说，你是一个更广阔的人，你可以利用更多的文化和更多的地方等等。现在，人们被锁定在共享资源中，比如租赁单元——不是租赁单元，而是分时度假公寓。最终他们厌倦了这一点，想要摆脱它，所有涉及文书工作等的旅行都是他们不喜欢的。但尽管如此，原则上，通过共享经济，你比必须自己购买所有这些东西拥有了更广泛的资源访问权限。

39
00:25:38.000 --> 00:26:08.000
Let's talk about another aspect of ownership, which is what happens when we have to give up things that, have been meaningful to us. Listen to an interview, you did where you talked about how helping your mother downsize changed your perspective somewhat on the meaning of possessions. Can you talk about that? I mean, how do we disentangle ourselves from things that have all of this meaning and help define who we are? Well, I'll come back to my mother.
让我们谈谈所有权的另一个方面，那就是当我们不得不放弃对我们有意义的东西时会发生什么。听过你的一次采访，你谈到帮助母亲精简生活在某种程度上改变了你对拥有物意义的看法。你能谈谈那个吗？我的意思是，我们如何将自己与那些具有所有这些意义并帮助定义我们是谁的东西分离开来？嗯，我会回到我母亲的话题。

40
00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:37.000
It's a sensitive area of still for me. But if you think about children's toys, for example, and children's art productions from going to school, these are things that we save oftentimes. And at some point, after the children have grown up or left home, we realize that we may need to downsize or may simply need to clean up the clutter in our house. And we can't get rid of these things immediately
这对我来说仍然是一个敏感的领域。但如果你想想儿童玩具，例如，以及孩子们从学校带回来的艺术作品，这些往往是我们保存的东西。在某个时候，孩子们长大或离家后，我们意识到我们可能需要精简生活，或者可能只是需要清理房子里的杂物。我们不能立即摆脱这些东西

41
00:26:37.000 --> 00:27:07.000
because they're so infused with memories. and they're hot possessions, if you will. And so if we put them away for a while in the attic or in the basement, these are places that are sort of liminal spaces where they can cool off a bit. And so when we've been distanced from them, they may not be as rich and saturated with memories as they were previously. Now, I found something similar with my mother, except that I live in Toronto and she lived in Minneapolis.
因为它们充满了记忆，如果可以的话，它们是“热的拥有物”。所以如果我们把它们在阁楼或地下室里放一段时间，这些地方是某种过渡空间，它们可以在那里冷却一下。所以当我们与它们保持距离后，它们可能不像以前那样充满记忆了。现在，我在我母亲身上发现了类似的情况，只是我住在多伦多，而她住在明尼阿波利斯。

42
00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:46.000
I'm the only surviving relative. And so I had limited time available to dispossess her of all of these former possessions. Let me go through the stages, I guess. First, she moved from a house with a lot of space and a lot of yard to keep up. And after my father died, it got to be too much for her. So she moved into an adult community where people helped each other, but it wasn't really a care facility. Then she did move into a care facility. Now, at each of those stages, she had to downsize, and we had to jointly decide what to keep and what not to keep.
我是唯一的幸存亲属。所以我只有有限的时间来处理她所有的这些前拥有物。让我经历一下这些阶段吧。首先，她从一个空间很大、院子很大的房子搬了出来。我父亲去世后，这对她来说太吃力了。所以她搬进了一个成人社区，人们互相帮助，但这并不是真正的护理机构。然后她确实搬进了一个护理机构。现在，在每一个阶段，她都必须精简，我们必须共同决定保留什么，不保留什么。

43
00:27:46.000 --> 00:28:44.000
